        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "吵 (chǎo) is inherited from Middle Chinese tsrhaewX (Guangyun, in Baxter's transcription), glossed 'sound', a different word from the chǎo of stir-frying, which has the same old form. The dictionary notes that the character first had another reading, mjiewX, glossed 'the call of a pheasant'."
